WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:30.919
THE THREAT OF HUAWEI:
Have you ever heard of company called Huawei?

00:00:30.919 --> 00:00:35.430
Inside the US, you probably haven’t heard
of them unless you’re a tech enthusiast.

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:41.340
But, outside the US and especially in Asia,
they ginormous.

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:47.410
In fact, a few years ago, they overtook Apple
and became the world’s second largest smartphone

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:50.390
seller, only beaten out by Samsung.

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:55.179
And during the pandemic, they furthered their
lead by overtaking Samsung as well.

00:00:55.179 --> 00:01:00.960
While this is awesome for Huawei and China,
it may not be all that great for the rest

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:02.250
of us.

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:06.910
If you’ve been following international news,
you probably know that Huwaei has a sketchy

00:01:06.910 --> 00:01:10.380
past when it comes to surveillance and data
collection.

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:16.890
Now, this is nothing new given that basically
every tech company does this, but there’s

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:21.140
one key difference between most tech companies
and Huawei.

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:25.850
Most companies collect your data to craft
effective marketing campaigns and sell you

00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:26.850
stuff.

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:31.710
Conversely, Huawei allegedly collects your
data for the Chinese Communist Party.

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:37.800
In fact, in 2019, the US actually outright
banned Huawei in the country.

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:43.409
Since then, tensions had been cooling down,
and Huawei was getting a break.

00:01:43.409 --> 00:01:47.850
But more recently, the pendulum has swung
in the other direction once again.

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:53.630
In late 2022, the US charged two Chinese intelligence
officers for trying to steal data regarding

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:55.460
the case against Huawei.

00:01:55.460 --> 00:02:00.270
Technically, we don’t know if this case
has to do with Huawei.

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:05.799
The US only disclosed that it had to do with
a large Chinese telecommunications company,

00:02:05.799 --> 00:02:09.250
but it’s assumed that they’re probably
talking about Huawei.

00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:13.320
It’s not just court cases that they’re
apparently spying on either.

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:18.780
The US accuses that China is using Huawei
to also spy on radio communications from military

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:21.940
bases, missile silos, and who knows what else.

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:27.190
And with Huawei becoming the largest smartphone
producer in the world, this threat is only

00:02:27.190 --> 00:02:28.840
growing larger by the day.

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:34.430
So, here’s how Huawei rose to overtake Samsung
and Apple, and how they could be using their

00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:38.390
marketshare to spy on the world for China.

00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:44.739
PROTECTING CHINA:
Taking a look back, the story of Huawei dates

00:02:44.739 --> 00:02:52.280
back to September of 1987 to a man named . At
the time, China was desperately trying to

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:55.170
develop their technology and economy.

00:02:55.170 --> 00:03:00.060
For the past few decades, China had fallen
quite behind due to internal conflict and

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:01.750
the communist revolution.

00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:07.580
But, moving into the 1980s, China wanted to
change that by embracing the free market to

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:08.830
a degree.

00:03:08.830 --> 00:03:12.880
One of these efforts centered on trying to
develop China’s telecommunications infrastructure.

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:18.220
Ren, a former deputy director of the People’s
Liberation Army engineering corps, was familiar

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:21.530
with the effort and decided to leverage the
situation.

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:26.760
He got together a couple of investors and
raised a total of $5000 to start Huawei.

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:31.790
You might be thinking that $5000 isn’t all
that much money especially to develop new

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:35.549
telecommunications technology, and you’d
be right.

00:03:35.549 --> 00:03:41.030
The point to note though is that Ren had no
intention of developing anything himself.

00:03:41.030 --> 00:03:47.250
Instead, his plan was to acquire foreign tech
and reverse engineer it using local researchers.

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:52.190
And when I say reverse engineer, I don’t
mean trying to understand the tech and creating

00:03:52.190 --> 00:03:53.590
something new.

00:03:53.590 --> 00:03:57.430
I mean blatantly ripping off competitors and
creating counterfeits.

00:03:57.430 --> 00:04:02.769
Ren’s first target was a new telecommunications
startup called Qualcomm, you may have heard

00:04:02.769 --> 00:04:04.069
of them.

00:04:04.069 --> 00:04:08.420
It turned out that trying to copy technology
itself took quite a bit of time.

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:13.420
So, to start bringing in some revenue, Ren
started to resell private branch exchange

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:16.889
or PBX switches that he imported from Hong
Kong.

00:04:16.889 --> 00:04:20.669
This revenue allowed the company to grow their
reverse engineering team substantially up

00:04:20.669 --> 00:04:22.440
to 600 people.

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:26.160
In terms of a company name, Ren simply settled
on a slogan that he saw that would play well

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:27.630
with the public.

00:04:27.630 --> 00:04:33.449
The original slogan was Zhonghua youwei which
means “China has promise”.

00:04:33.449 --> 00:04:38.789
Ren altered to this Huawei or as it’s properly
pronounced Wah-Way which translates to “China

00:04:38.789 --> 00:04:40.130
is able.”

00:04:40.130 --> 00:04:44.540
The CCP was likely fans of this name, but
that’s not the only thing that attracted

00:04:44.540 --> 00:04:45.630
them to Huawei.

00:04:45.630 --> 00:04:50.960
By 1993, the company would successfully create
their own telephone switch knockoff which

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:53.810
was magnitudes better than anything else in
China.

00:04:53.810 --> 00:04:58.550
Huawei turned around and deployed these switches
across small cities and rural areas where

00:04:58.550 --> 00:05:01.300
there wasn’t much competition from the existing
players.

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:06.130
But, likely their biggest and smartest move
was partnering with the People's Liberation

00:05:06.130 --> 00:05:07.190
Army.

00:05:07.190 --> 00:05:11.940
In terms of revenue, this deal wasn’t all
that lucrative, but in terms of connections

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:15.470
and influence, this deal was insane.

00:05:15.470 --> 00:05:19.789
The story goes that when Ren was pitching
the CCP on telephone switch, he told them

00:05:19.789 --> 00:05:24.870
quote “switching equipment technology was
related to national security, and that a nation

00:05:24.870 --> 00:05:29.560
that did not have its own switching equipment
was like one that lacked its own military."

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:35.770
Reportedly, the CCP official agreed, but if
that’s the case, why in the world is Huawei

00:05:35.770 --> 00:05:39.569
trying to get other countries to use their
telecommunications equip.

00:05:39.569 --> 00:05:43.570
Yeah, I think you can see where this is going.

00:05:43.570 --> 00:05:49.680
INFILTRATING COUNTRIES:
It appears that China truly did believe in

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:54.259
Ren’s statement as they would triple down
on Huawei and domestic switches.

00:05:54.259 --> 00:06:01.060
In fact, in 1996, China adopted an explicit
policy that supported domestic manufacturing

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:03.949
and restricted access to foreign options.

00:06:03.949 --> 00:06:07.990
Given that Huawei was the only Chinese company
that was producing equipment that was comparable

00:06:07.990 --> 00:06:12.720
to foreign counterparts, Huawei became the
go to company for both the government and

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:14.120
the military.

00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:17.590
They weren’t just making strides within
China either.

00:06:17.590 --> 00:06:22.270
Starting in the late 1990s, Huawei would aggressively
expand to developing countries in Sub-Saharan

00:06:22.270 --> 00:06:24.660
Africa and the Middle East.

00:06:24.660 --> 00:06:29.960
These countries were easy targets given that
most of them couldn’t afford western versions.

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:33.289
So, there was really only two options.

00:06:33.289 --> 00:06:37.180
Either you take the help from the Chinese
or you put off development.

00:06:37.180 --> 00:06:40.080
Obviously, they went with the first option.

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:44.349
What’s surprising though is that western
countries would soon jump onto the Huawei

00:06:44.349 --> 00:06:45.889
bandwagon as well.

00:06:45.889 --> 00:06:50.910
One of the first countries to fall was Canada
when their telecom giant Nortel outsourced

00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:54.220
their entire production and engineering to
Huawei.

00:06:54.220 --> 00:06:58.289
It appears that higher profit margins was
simply something that many western companies

00:06:58.289 --> 00:07:00.000
couldn’t turn down.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:05.180
Don’t be fooled though, switching to Huawei
was usually a terrible decision.

00:07:05.180 --> 00:07:10.970
And it’s not even a conspiracy theory as
its usually blatantly obvious, though Huawei

00:07:10.970 --> 00:07:12.990
of course always denies everything.

00:07:12.990 --> 00:07:15.160
Take Nortel for example.

00:07:15.160 --> 00:07:19.819
Shortly after making a deal with Huawei, Chinese
hackers would begin stealing hundreds of sensitive

00:07:19.819 --> 00:07:25.039
internal documents from Nortel for a period
of atleast 10 years.

00:07:25.039 --> 00:07:29.430
According to Huawei, this hacking has nothing
to do with Nortel’s deal with them, but

00:07:29.430 --> 00:07:31.650
you can take that for what you will.

00:07:31.650 --> 00:07:36.490
To make things worse, it wasn’t just Nortel
that Huawei was allegedly spying on.

00:07:36.490 --> 00:07:41.169
Huawei may have gained access to Canada’s
National Defence through Nortel.

00:07:41.169 --> 00:07:46.710
Reportedly, listening devices were found in
Nortel’s Ottawa R&amp;D complex.

00:07:46.710 --> 00:07:51.849
This complex worked extremely closely with
Canada’s national defence, so much so that

00:07:51.849 --> 00:07:56.520
after Nortel filed for bankruptcy largely
due to corporate espionage, this building

00:07:56.520 --> 00:07:59.690
would become Canada’s national defence HQ.

00:07:59.690 --> 00:08:05.630
For obvious reasons, Canada isn’t letting
Huawei come anywhere near their 5G networks.

00:08:05.630 --> 00:08:09.520
Canada wasn’t the only country to learn
the hard way.

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:13.539
Around the same time that Nortel signed their
deal with Huawei, dozens of western companies

00:08:13.539 --> 00:08:16.450
were signing deals with them as well.

00:08:16.450 --> 00:08:20.949
At first, this started off with background
players like UK’s Vodafone, Australia’s

00:08:20.949 --> 00:08:24.199
Optus, Norway’s Telenor, and Japan’s Panasonic.

00:08:24.199 --> 00:08:28.700
But, this quickly grew to encompass all of
the American giants as well including Google,

00:08:28.700 --> 00:08:31.820
Facebook, Qualcomm, Intel, and Microsoft.

00:08:31.820 --> 00:08:38.190
Since then, each and every one of the companies
that I just listed have cut their ties with

00:08:38.190 --> 00:08:42.169
Huawei either due to getting burned themselves
or due to government bans.

00:08:42.169 --> 00:08:47.279
So, fortunately, it looks like companies and
countries started wising up to Huawei in the

00:08:47.279 --> 00:08:48.670
late 2010s.

00:08:48.670 --> 00:08:55.260
But, this didn’t matter given that Huawei
had already shifted to a completely different

00:08:55.260 --> 00:08:56.820
ball game.

00:08:56.820 --> 00:09:02.740
INFILTRATING THE WORLD:
From the very beginning, Huawei knew that

00:09:02.740 --> 00:09:05.720
the endgame was to reach end users.

00:09:05.720 --> 00:09:09.510
They couldn’t quite do this from the very
beginning given that creating consumer tech

00:09:09.510 --> 00:09:12.220
and reaching everyday people is expensive.

00:09:12.220 --> 00:09:17.080
But, thanks to all of these contracts with
companies and governments, Huawei was able

00:09:17.080 --> 00:09:19.640
to infiltrate the consumer market.

00:09:19.640 --> 00:09:24.450
And given that they were a telecommunications
company, their product was obviously cell

00:09:24.450 --> 00:09:25.450
phones.

00:09:25.450 --> 00:09:27.900
Huawei didn’t actually start with smartphones.

00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:33.020
In fact, their first cell phone, the C300
came out way back in 2003.

00:09:33.020 --> 00:09:39.830
They followed this up with a 3G phone called
the U626 and a 3G handset called the V710.

00:09:39.830 --> 00:09:45.140
These products performed alright in China
and some parts of Europe, but Huawei’s breakthrough

00:09:45.140 --> 00:09:49.580
didn’t come until the introduction of smartphones
which they were perfectly positioned for.

00:09:49.580 --> 00:09:54.750
They were already in the telecommunications
market, they were already in the cellphone

00:09:54.750 --> 00:10:00.660
market, they had full backing from the CCP,
and they had an international presence.

00:10:00.660 --> 00:10:06.260
Also, the company was mature enough where
they didn’t have directly copy competitors.

00:10:06.260 --> 00:10:11.410
Instead, they could take inspiration and incorporate
their own twist which is exactly what they

00:10:11.410 --> 00:10:14.890
did with the U8220 in 2009.

00:10:14.890 --> 00:10:19.260
Since then, Huawei has become a leader when
it comes to incorporating the latest networks,

00:10:19.260 --> 00:10:23.000
cameras, charging, and even designs.

00:10:23.000 --> 00:10:27.810
And the reason that their smartphones are
so popular isn’t rocket science.

00:10:27.810 --> 00:10:33.200
They make great phones that are usually cheaper
than the competition, it’s that simple.

00:10:33.200 --> 00:10:38.100
And today, they regularly ship more than 200
million phones every single year.

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:42.490
I don’t think you’d be surprised to hear
that they’ve expanded to selling much than

00:10:42.490 --> 00:10:43.690
smartphones either.

00:10:43.690 --> 00:10:48.640
They’ve got laptops, tablets, watches, and
even a car.

00:10:48.640 --> 00:10:55.870
So, Huawei isn’t going anywhere anytime
soon, but what does this mean for all of us?

00:10:55.870 --> 00:11:00.550
Well, whether Huawei wants to admit it or
not, they’re likely going to spy on and

00:11:00.550 --> 00:11:02.570
surveil as many people as they can.

00:11:02.570 --> 00:11:08.170
Many people who buy Huawei products are well
aware of this, but they don’t really care.

00:11:08.170 --> 00:11:12.769
They usually figure that even if it’s not
Huawei that’s spying on them, it’ll be

00:11:12.769 --> 00:11:15.310
companies like Google, Facebook, and Oracle.

00:11:15.310 --> 00:11:19.990
So, they might as well forget about trying
to protect their privacy and instead just

00:11:19.990 --> 00:11:22.940
focusing on buying the best product for the
best price.

00:11:22.940 --> 00:11:28.839
So, that answers the consumer side of things,
but what about Huawei’s side of things:

00:11:28.839 --> 00:11:31.649
Why spy in the first place?.

00:11:31.649 --> 00:11:39.329
WHY SPY?:
Clearly, Huawei is extraordinarily popular,

00:11:39.329 --> 00:11:42.760
so why resort to allegedly spying for the
CCP?

00:11:42.760 --> 00:11:49.339
At the end of the day, they’re a hardware
company not a software company, so they shouldn’t

00:11:49.339 --> 00:11:51.240
need to collect data right?

00:11:51.240 --> 00:11:54.680
Well, the answer is that they have no choice.

00:11:54.680 --> 00:12:01.450
It was the CCP who made Huawei into who they
are today and CCP can bring back Huawei to

00:12:01.450 --> 00:12:03.579
0 if they wanted to.

00:12:03.579 --> 00:12:09.190
And if you don’t believe me, just look at
Jack Ma and Alibaba.

00:12:09.190 --> 00:12:15.450
Over the years, the CCP has provided Huawei
with $46 billion in loans along with $25 billion

00:12:15.450 --> 00:12:17.040
tax cuts.

00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:22.110
And who knows how much revenue the CCP has
brought Huawei in the form of government contracts.

00:12:22.110 --> 00:12:27.220
Also, we should not that this has always been
a one sided relationship.

00:12:27.220 --> 00:12:32.680
While Huawei is dependent on the CCP, the
CCP is not dependent on Huawei.

00:12:32.680 --> 00:12:38.990
They CCP didn’t put all of their eggs in
one basket as they also have giants like Tencent,

00:12:38.990 --> 00:12:44.470
JD.com, Alibaba, TikTok, and the entirety
of the Chinese financial system.

00:12:44.470 --> 00:12:50.830
So, China basically owns Huawei, and Huawei
has to do what the CCP says regardless of

00:12:50.830 --> 00:12:54.440
how that will affect their own reputation
or public image.

00:12:54.440 --> 00:12:59.020
Looking forward, while countries are doing
their best to hamper Huawei’s influence,

00:12:59.020 --> 00:13:03.880
it’s not easy to mitigate the control you
get from having access to billions of users

00:13:03.880 --> 00:13:07.170
and decades of telecommunications infrastructure.

00:13:07.170 --> 00:13:12.520
And given that most consumers just see Huawei
as another Facebook or Google, it’s unlikely

00:13:12.520 --> 00:13:15.660
that we’ll see much resistance from the
general public either.

00:13:15.660 --> 00:13:20.880
So, its safe to say that Huawei wont have
much trouble maintaining their dominance and

00:13:20.880 --> 00:13:23.870
allegedly reporting their findings to CCP.

00:13:23.870 --> 00:13:27.660
Would you rather give your data to Huawei
or Facebook?

